export const CREATION_CHAPTERS: Record<string, string> = {
  "angels-in-islam-ch3": "# Angels in Islam\n\nBeyond what the eyes can measure, Islam describes a creation that is real, ordered, and constantly active—yet hidden from ordinary sight. This chapter explores the [[Malaika|malaika]]: who they are, why belief in them matters, and how revelation speaks about their roles in the world.\n\n## Belief in the unseen\n\nAfter the creation of the skies and the earth, revelation speaks about another creation of Allah: the [[Malaika|malaika]]—angels.\n\nThey were created long before humanity, and they are not normally visible to us. Yet belief in angels is presented as a pillar of [[Imaan|imaan]].\n\nThe [[Qur’an|quran]] mentions this more than once, and it treats belief in angels as part of what makes faith complete.\n\n## Why angels cannot be seen\n\nA natural question follows: if angels exist, why are they not seen?\n\nThe Qur’an draws a line between what can be witnessed and what remains unseen. Human sight is not a universal instrument—it is a narrow window.\n\nSo the question becomes less about “where are they?” and more about “what can humans actually detect?”\n\n## What science can detect—and what it cannot\n\nIn the physical world, anything that can be observed directly or indirectly leaves traces—signals, waves, or measurable effects. Modern science describes a wide range of such signals as the [[electromagnetic spectrum|electromagnetic-spectrum]].\n\nBut even within that spectrum, human eyesight only captures a tiny portion: the [[visible spectrum|visible-spectrum]].\n\nIf creation contains realities outside that narrow band, then not seeing something does not automatically mean it does not exist. It may simply mean it is not accessible to the tools humans possess.\n\n## What angels are created from\n\nIn reported tradition, the [[Hadith|hadith]] describe the origins of different creations:\n\n\\- Angels are described as created from [[Noor|noor]]—a light.\n\n\\- The [[Jinn|jinn]] are described as created from the flame of smokeless fire.\n\n\\- Adam is described as created from earth.\n\nThis chapter presents these as transmitted descriptions of origin—part of how the unseen is introduced to the human mind.\n\n## “Malaika” — what the word points to\n\nThe Qur’an uses the word *malaika*.\n\nIn this narration, the term is linked to meanings associated with authority, command, and the carrying out of orders—angels as a creation that moves under divine instruction, never acting independently from Allah’s will.\n\n## Angels with humans from the womb to the grave\n\nThe narration describes angels assigned to a human being from the earliest stages of life.\n\nA fetus is described as being attended by an angel who is commanded with matters of decree—recording and carrying out what Allah wills for that life.\n\nFrom that point onward, the chapter describes angels who remain close to human beings through different phases: living, dying, and what follows after burial.\n\n## The noble recorders: [[Kiraman Katibeen|kiraman-katibeen]]\n\nThe Qur’an speaks about honored recorders—angels who record deeds.\n\nIn this narration, [[Kiraman Katibeen|kiraman-katibeen]] are described as two angels who remain with a person, recording actions and words.\n\nThe point emphasized here is accountability: nothing significant is lost, even when it seems unseen by people.\n\n## The guardians: [[Mu’aqabaat|muaqabaat]]\n\nAnother category is mentioned in connection with protection—angels assigned around a person.\n\nIn Surah Ra’d, the narration references [[Mu’aqabaat|muaqabaat]] as those positioned “in front and behind,” guarding by Allah’s command.\n\nProtection is described here as real, but not absolute: it exists within the limits of divine decree.\n\n## The angel of death—and those under his command\n\nThe narration then turns to death.\n\nIt references the angel of death, known here as [[Izraeel|izraeel]], described as being assigned for taking souls at the appointed time.\n\nIt also describes angels working under this command—carrying out what is written, without delay and without injustice.\n\n## The angels of the grave: [[Munkar and Nakir|munkar-and-nakir]]\n\nAfter burial, the narration describes the visit of two angels of questioning: [[Munkar and Nakir|munkar-and-nakir]].\n\nIn this framing, the grave is not presented as “silence,” but as a beginning—where a person faces questions about belief, truth, and allegiance.\n\n## The sky is filled with them\n\nThe chapter emphasizes scale: angels are not described as rare. The heavens are described as filled with them.\n\nThe narration presents angels as a vast creation—occupying stations in the skies, engaged in worship, carrying tasks, and moving through pathways beyond human sight.\n\n## Angels act only by Allah’s command\n\nA consistent theme repeats: angels do not rebel, and they do not improvise against divine will.\n\nThey are described as a creation that carries out commands—perfectly, precisely, and without the moral weakness found in human beings.\n\n## Angels appearing as humans\n\nThe Qur’an describes angels appearing to people, and the chapter highlights a pattern:\n\nWhen angels interacted with non-prophets, the narration emphasizes that they appeared in human form.\n\nExamples referenced include:\n\n\\- angels interacting with Prophet Ibrahim (a.s.) in Surah Hijr\n\n\\- angels interacting with Prophet Lut (a.s.) in Surah Hijr\n\n\\- glad tidings delivered to Prophet Zakariya (a.s.)\n\n\\- glad tidings delivered to Maryam (a.s.)\n\n\\- glad tidings delivered to Sarah, the wife of Prophet Ibrahim (a.s.)\n\nThe emphasis here is restraint and mercy: an overwhelming sight is not imposed on the human heart.\n\n## When angels enter—and when they do not\n\nThe narration then mentions conditions connected to angels entering homes.\n\nIt reports that angels do not enter a house where there are pictures or dogs.\n\nIt also describes special occasions: moments where angels descend for unique tasks, and moments where an angel descended with glad tidings of revelation.\n\nOne example described is the descent of an angel who had not descended before, delivering good news connected to Surah Al-Fatihah and the closing verses of Surah Al-Baqarah.\n\nAnother example mentioned is the report connected to Umm Salama (r.a.), where the home was prepared for an angel who broughtattachers of a future event and a sign of the land where it would occur.\n\n## Pathways of ascent and descent\n\nThe chapter describes structured movement: angels ascending and descending through assigned pathways.\n\nThe unseen is not described as chaos. It is described as an ordered realm—where decrees move, records are carried, and commands are executed.\n\n## Names mentioned—and mysteries that remain\n\nSome angels are named in narration, while many remain unnamed.\n\nThe chapter treats named angels as signposts—enough to understand roles and patterns—while acknowledging that the unseen contains more than what is disclosed.\n\n## Angels described in the skies\n\nThe narration describes angels stationed in different skies and describes extraordinary forms connected to those stations.\n\nThese descriptions are presented as reports of the unseen—meant to communicate power, scale, and the limits of the human imagination.\n\n## [[Malik|malik-angel]], [[Zabaniyya|zabaniyya]], and a terrifying warning\n\nThe narration speaks about the angels connected with punishment.\n\nIt describes [[Malik|malik-angel]] as the custodian connected to Hell, and it references the [[Zabaniyya|zabaniyya]] as angels associated with severe enforcement.\n\nThe lesson emphasized is not spectacle—it is seriousness: actions have consequences, and the unseen is not a metaphor.\n\n## [[Bayt al-Ma’moor|bayt-al-mamoor]]: the much-frequented house\n\nThe narration references [[Bayt al-Ma’moor|bayt-al-mamoor]] as a sacred station in the heavens.\n\nIt is described as a place constantly visited—an image meant to convey that worship in the heavens is continuous, ordered, and immense in number.\n\n## [[Mikael|mikael]] and [[Israfeel|israfeel]]\n\nThe chapter then names angels associated with immense functions in the world:\n\n\\- [[Mikael|mikael]] is referenced in connection with provision and the ordering of worldly processes.\n\n\\- [[Israfeel|israfeel]] is referenced in connection with the trumpet and the coming of the final event.\n\nThese are presented as roles within a vast structure of command.\n\n## [[Jibraeel|jibraeel]]: the messenger of revelation\n\nThe narration places special emphasis on [[Jibraeel|jibraeel]].\n\nHe is described as the angel of revelation—connected directly to the delivery of divine message to prophets.\n\nThe chapter also references his appearance in human form, including reports linking this to [[Dihyah bin Khalifah al-Kalbi|dihyah-bin-khalifah-al-kalbi]].\n\n## [[Ra’d|rad-angel]] — and a question from the Jews\n\nThe narration references [[Ra’d|rad-angel]] in connection with thunder and a reported question.\n\nIt presents this as part of the broader theme: even forces humans treat as “nature” can be described, within the unseen framework, as acting under command.\n\n## How do angels look?\n\nPopular imagination often turns angels into gentle symbols.\n\nBut the narration presented here describes angels in far broader ways—some awe-inspiring, some terrifying, and some beyond description.\n\nThe Qur’an mentions angels with wings and speaks of Allah increasing creation as He wills.\n\nThe chapter also references descriptions of angels of questioning and angels of punishment in forms that do not match soft human fantasy.\n\n## Descriptions across traditions\n\nThe chapter briefly compares descriptions found across different traditions.\n\nTerms like [[Cherubim|cherubim]], [[Seraphim|seraphim]], and [[Ophanim|ophanim]] are referenced as examples of how other religious literatures speak about heavenly beings.\n\nA historical location—[[Hagia Sophia|hagia-sophia]]—is mentioned in connection with how religious symbolism and imperial history carry echoes of earlier sacred language.\n\nThis section is presented as comparison, not as confirmation: the anchor of the chapter remains Qur’an and transmitted Islamic reports.\n\n## The carriers of the Throne—and those nearest to it\n\nThe chapter mentions angels of the highest ranks.\n\nIt references categories described as nearest—[[Muqarraboun|muqarraboun]]—and references the highest recorded realm as [[Illiyyeen|illiyyeen]].\n\nThe purpose of these references is scale and hierarchy: a layered unseen world, with proximity to mercy and proximity to command.\n\n## The edge of what can be known\n\nAt the end of these descriptions, the chapter presses a boundary:\n\nSome matters are disclosed because they guide belief and character. Other matters remain unknown because humans are not meant to carry them.\n\nWhere revelation speaks, the narration follows. Where revelation is silent, the chapter discourages certainty.\n\n## A scientific lens: the [[Kardashev Scale|kardashev-scale]]\n\nThe chapter then introduces a modern comparison—not as proof, but as perspective.\n\nIn 1964, during the [[Byurakan conference|byurakan-conference]], a scientist named [[Nikolai Kardashev|nikolai-kardashev]] presented what became known as the [[Kardashev Scale|kardashev-scale]]—a way of imagining how civilizations might be measured by the energy they can harness.\n\n### Type 1, Type 2, Type 3\n\nIn this framing:\n\n\\- a Type 1 civilization is described as harnessing planetary-scale energy\n\n\\- a Type 2 civilization is described as harnessing star-scale energy\n\n\\- a Type 3 civilization is described as harnessing galaxy-scale energy\n\nThis chapter uses the comparison to highlight humility: even humanity’s highest imagination remains small.\n\n## A real-life example: [[first contact|first-contact]]\n\nA concept called [[first contact|first-contact]] is referenced—what happens when two peoples meet for the first time, previously unaware of each other’s existence.\n\nThe example given describes a meeting in 1930 involving indigenous people in Papua New Guinea encountering a Caucasian man for the first time.\n\nThe chapter uses this to underline a psychological point: human hearts can fear what is unfamiliar—even when the unfamiliar is merely another human.\n\nSo how would the heart handle a mighty angel in true form?\n\n## The second sighting of [[Jibraeel|jibraeel]]\n\nThe chapter ends by referencing the second sighting of [[Jibraeel|jibraeel]], linked to verses in Surah Najm and to reports of the night of ascension.\n\nIt describes the Prophet ﷺ witnessing immense signs—among them the great lote tree, [[Sidrat al-Muntaha|sidra-al-muntaha]], described here as stretching between the sixth and seventh heavens.\n\nIn this narration, [[Jibraeel|jibraeel]] is described in his true form, with vast wings that cover the horizons—an image meant to convey magnitude beyond human scale.\n\nThe chapter frames this moment as a peak of awe: creation bowing before command, and the Messenger ﷺ witnessing signs that are beyond ordinary description.\n\n## A closing narration: angels who seek gatherings of remembrance\n\nA final hadith is presented about angels who travel the earth, seeking gatherings where Allah is remembered.\n\nThe narration describes angels joining such gatherings, then ascending and reporting to Allah—who already knows—what His servants were doing.\n\nIt describes mercy being written for those present, and even for someone who came for a different purpose yet sat among the people of remembrance.\n\nThe chapter closes on that note: unseen mercy can surround a gathering, and blessing can reach those who simply choose to sit among those who remember Allah.\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
  "creation-of-the-universe-ch2": "# The Creation of the Seven Skies\n\nThis chapter begins at the edge of what the human eye can imagine: a universe so vast that even its earliest light is still arriving. From modern discovery to ancient memory, it asks one question again and again—what is “the sky,” and what lies beyond it?\n\n## A journey that starts at the farthest light\n\nEvery civilization has looked upward and felt the same tension: wonder, fear, and longing for meaning.\n\nIf you trace the night sky with patience, you may notice a familiar pattern—[[Ursa Major|ursa-major]]. And if your sight were the most powerful telescope ever built, you could detect a tiny red signature near that region: [[GN-z11|gn-z11]].\n\nThat distant glow represents an astonishing fact. Light—moving as fast as anything we know—can still take **14 billion years** to reach us. If that is true, then this journey can reach at least that far back in time.\n\nAnd yet almost all of that timespan is before us. Human life appears only at the very end of the cosmological scale. So how can the beginning be “seen” at all?\n\nNot by eyesight—but by knowledge.\n\n## Science, cosmogony, and a discovery in 1964\n\nA modern branch of study called [[Cosmogony|cosmogony]] focuses on the origins of the universe. In 1964, two scientists—[[Arno Penzias|arno-penzias]] and [[Robert Wilson|robert-wilson]]—encountered something unexpected: a faint, persistent radiation that did not seem to come from any star or planet.\n\nIt appeared everywhere they looked, like a quiet background spread through the whole cosmos—an after-effect of a great event.\n\nThis discovery became known as the [[Cosmic Microwave Background|cosmic-microwave-background]].\n\nFrom here, the modern story is often summarized as [[the Big Bang|big-bang]]: that the heavens and the earth, and all that lies between, were once together—and then separated by a tremendous expansion whose echoes remain.\n\n## The horizon problem and the limits of science\n\nBut the discovery also raised a troubling question, often described as the [[horizon problem|horizon-problem]].\n\nThe issue is presented like this: the background radiation appears across vast distances, but not always in the same way everywhere. This can seem to imply that something about the universe behaves differently in different regions.\n\nAt this point, a broader truth becomes unavoidable: science is powerful, but it also has limits. It measures what it can reach. And beyond that boundary, other kinds of knowledge have always been sought.\n\nSo the search turns backward—to the ancient world.\n\n## Ancient tablets and the memory of creation\n\nIn 1849, in the region of [[Nineveh|nineveh]], an archaeologist discovered clay [[tablets|ancient-tablets]]—the kind used for writing thousands of years ago.\n\nThese writings became known as [[Enuma Elish|enuma-elish]]. Their creation narrative speaks of a time before sky and earth, when everything was water, and destiny had not yet been written—until there was an order from God.\n\nAcross the ancient world, creation stories often begin this way: with water, with a command, and with a world shaped from what was already “there.”\n\nA Greek term used for such an initiating command is [[logos|logos]]—something spoken, something declared.\n\n## Many traditions, many beginnings\n\nIf you gather the major traditions of the world, you find a wide range of creation concepts—some cosmic, some symbolic, some purely mythic, and some presented as literal history.\n\nAncient Mesopotamian memory, for example, includes the [[Sumerians|sumerians]] and accounts tied to primordial water beings such as [[Nammu|nammu]]. Egyptian memory contains the idea of [[Nun|nun-egypt]]. Greek imagination speaks of [[Okeanos|okeanos]] as a vast surrounding sea. Even northern epics mention figures like [[Väinämöinen|vainamoinen]].\n\nOther traditions describe the universe as timeless—without a beginning or end.\n\nAnd in some narratives, cosmology becomes tangled: stories of a flat world, of short timelines, or of the heavens rotating around the earth. The point is not mockery—it is contrast. When a claim is presented as truth, it can be tested by what the world itself displays.\n\nEven modern observation—satellites in orbit, the movement of day and night across the globe—forces the question: which descriptions can survive the pressure of reality?\n\n## Islam, the Qur’an, and a claim that invites testing\n\nNow the narrative turns to one tradition that presents itself with an unusually direct claim.\n\nAbout 1400 years ago, a religion emerged in Arabia: [[Islam|islam]]. Its central scripture, the [[Qur’an|quran]], presents a creation framework that also speaks of the heavens and the earth being created in stages, and of water being central.\n\nIt also presents a striking statement near its beginning:\n\n> “It is a book in which there is no doubt.”\n\nThis is not a casual sentence. It is a claim—and claims can be examined.\n\nThe Qur’an also directs attention to the Prophet’s teachings preserved in [[Hadith|hadith]], where additional details about creation and the unseen are discussed.\n\n## A Qur’anic challenge and echoes in the universe\n\nOne of the Qur’an’s creation statements appears in *[[Surah Al-Anbiya|surah-al-anbiya]]*:\n\n> “Do disbelievers not see that the earth and sky were together… and We separated them… and We created all living things from water.”\n\nWithin this chapter’s framing, the separation suggests a mighty expansion—an event whose traces still remain, like the background echo recognized in the [[Cosmic Microwave Background|cosmic-microwave-background]].\n\nIt also raises a further question that appears repeatedly in the Qur’an: the heavens and the earth were created in “six days.” What kind of “days” are meant? Are they like the 24-hour cycle people measure on earth—or something else?\n\nThe chapter continues with that tension: the universe seems older than any short timeline, and yet the language of scripture is purposeful, not casual.\n\n## Darkness and a 300-year-old question\n\nAnother Qur’anic statement appears early in *[[Surah Al-An’am|surah-al-anam]]*:\n\n> “All praise is to Allah who created skies and the earth—and the lights and the darknesses.”\n\nIt presents darkness not merely as absence, but as something “made.”\n\nThat idea intersects with an old astronomical puzzle raised by a 17th-century German thinker, [[Heinrich Olbers|heinrich-olbers]], known as [[Olbers’ paradox|olbers-paradox]]:  \nIf the universe were infinite and filled uniformly with stars, why is the night sky not blazing bright?\n\nIn this chapter’s voice, the night appears almost like a covering—something that veils light.\n\nA related Qur’anic expression is:\n\n> “I swear by the night when she covers.”\n\nThe point here is not that one verse “solves” a scientific paradox. The point is that the text repeatedly invites attention to the architecture of light and darkness.\n\n## What exactly is “the sky”?\n\nNow the question becomes precise.\n\nAsk people what “sky” means and you will hear many answers:\n- the blue expanse\n- the clouds\n- a distant ceiling\n- a protective shield above the world\n\nModern explanation begins with the atmosphere: what appears blue is air, light, and scattering. But the chapter presses further—because “sky” in scripture also includes stars, moons, and distant structures.\n\nSo the “sky” cannot be only clouds.\n\n## The layers above us\n\nScience divides the atmosphere into layers.\n\nA short distance above is the [[troposphere|troposphere]]—where weather lives: clouds, rain, storms, and the conditions that support life.\n\nAbove that is the [[stratosphere|stratosphere]], including what is popularly known as the [[ozone layer|ozone-layer]].\n\nAbove that is the [[mesosphere|mesosphere]], where air becomes thin and cold, and where objects entering from space can begin to burn.\n\nAbove that lies the [[thermosphere|thermosphere]], where many satellites orbit—and where shimmering lights appear, called [[aurora lights|aurora]].\n\nThen comes a conceptual boundary: the [[Kármán line|karman-line]]—often spoken of as the transition into outer space.\n\nBeyond it stretches a vast expansion whose scale overwhelms ordinary intuition.\n\n## Qur’anic descriptions of the sky\n\nThe Qur’an speaks repeatedly of water descending “from the sky”:\n\n> “We descended the water from the sky.”\n\nBut clouds are not the whole story.\n\nThe Qur’an also calls the sky a “safe roof,” and speaks of it as secured—suggesting protection and stability.\n\nIt describes patterns that return repeatedly, and links the sky with the sun, the moon, and the stars. It speaks of stars as decoration, and even mentions “pillars” within the sky.\n\nA verse from the chapter of stone describes:\n\n> “The beautiful pillars are placed in the sky, for those who see.”\n\nThe chapter then points to a famous image captured in 1995, known as the [[Pillars of Creation|pillars-of-creation]]—three towering structures photographed in deep space.\n\nThe claim made here is directional: the “sky” is not one thin layer. It is an upward expansion—beginning above us and stretching beyond imagination, with limits known only to Allah.\n\n## Expansion, elevation, and an outer boundary\n\nThe Qur’an’s references to the sky include more than visible stars. It speaks of phenomena that burn, chase, or consume—descriptions that suggest realities beyond ordinary sight.\n\nThe chapter emphasizes scale:\n- Our [[Milky Way|milky-way]] is described as having hundreds of billions of stars.\n- Nearby galaxies are described as containing even more.\n- Beyond them are countless galaxies spread across a vast expanse.\n\nSo the question returns: where does this “sky” end, and what is above it?\n\n## Reading the Qur’an for a pattern\n\nA careful reading of the Qur’an presents recurring themes about the sky:\n- design and structure\n- beings within it\n- things that descend and ascend\n- pathways and doors\n- and the destiny of the sky itself\n\nWithin this framing, the Qur’an also repeats a number: the skies are seven.\n\nSo the chapter now turns to that description.\n\n## The seven skies\n\nAt this point, the narrative leaves what can be measured and moves into what is described through Qur’an and reports.\n\nA 14th-century Egyptian scholar, [[Jalal al-Din al-Suyuti|jalal-al-din-al-suyuti]], is referenced alongside a work titled [[As-Samaya Fil Hayat As-Samiyyah|as-samaya-fil-hayat-as-samiyyah]], presented here as a source that gathers descriptions of the seven skies.\n\nThe description begins from the farthest edge humanity commonly speaks of—the [[Kármán line|karman-line]]—and continues upward.\n\n### Sky one: the first boundary\n\nThe first sky is described as the sky closest to earth—presented as part of the vast expansion above.\n\nIt is described as being connected with water and referred to here as “raqiya.” The chapter also links this to verses that mention smoke and fire as a response to anyone attempting to breach the boundary.\n\nThe [[Hadith|hadith]] of [[Isra and Mi’raj|isra-and-miraj]] is referenced here as describing encounters within the heavens.\n\n### A letter, a question, and “mujarra”\n\nA historical report is mentioned involving a Roman emperor and a letter sent to Mu’awiya, then forwarded to Ibn Abbas.\n\nThe letter is said to ask about “mujarra,” which Ibn Abbas describes as a kind of doorway or barrier in the sky—something separating “here” from “there,” with the implication that nothing passes through it easily, not even heat or temperature.\n\nThe chapter also presents a further idea: that this barrier could be described as a living liquid—water that is “alive.” The text notes this as a reported concept rather than something established by measurement.\n\n### Sky two to seven (as described)\n\nThe chapter continues with a layered description:\n\n- The second sky is described as being made of white pearls, called “aazfiloun,” and associated with Prophet Eissa and Yahya in the narration.\n- The third sky is described as being made of iron, called “qaidoum,” associated with Prophet Youssef and, in some traditions, the angel of death.\n- The fourth sky is described as being made of brass, called “maaou’na,” associated with Prophet Idrees and an angel described as the angel of tears.\n- The fifth sky is described as being made of silver, called “riqqa,” associated with Prophet Haroon, and with the custodian of Hell, Malik, who commands angels described as [[Zabaniyya|zabaniyya]].\n- The sixth sky is described as being made of gold and red stones, associated with Prophet Musa. A great tree is also mentioned here—the lote tree of the farthest boundary, commonly referred to as *[[Sidrat al-Muntaha|sidrat-al-muntaha]]*—with descriptions of vast leaves and extraordinary beauty.\n- The seventh sky is described as being made of light beyond ordinary understanding, associated with Prophet Ibrahim, and described with the name “areeba,” also linked here with “araboth” among the people of the Book.\n\nThese descriptions are presented in the chapter as narrations of the unseen, conveyed through reported tradition rather than observation.\n\n## Above the seven skies\n\nBeyond the seventh sky, the chapter describes:\n- the Throne, carried by eight great angels\n- a further “great chair”\n- and the limit of created knowledge\n\nIt describes a place where everything is written—*[[Loh-e-Mahfooz|loh-e-mahfooz]]*—with imagery of light, pearls, and rubies, and the idea of destiny recorded.\n\nIt also presents a scale comparison: the seven skies compared to the Throne, and the Throne compared to the great chair, described as rings in a vast desert—an image meant to humble the mind before the immensity of creation.\n\n## “Be” — and it becomes\n\nAcross many cultures, creation stories speak about a force and a command, but often leave the “words” unknown.\n\nThe chapter then points to a Qur’anic phrase repeated multiple times:\n\n> “When We wish something to be, We order it: ‘Be’—and so it becomes.”\n\nWithin the chapter’s framing, this is presented as the simplest expression of an origin that does not require material struggle: command, will, and existence.\n\n## A narration and a regret\n\nA narration is included about Imran bin Hussain visiting the Prophet ﷺ when people from Yemen asked:\n\n> “O Prophet of Allah, we have come to know from you—how this universe came into being.”\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ began describing Allah’s eternal existence, the Throne above water, and the writing of destiny—when a disturbance occurred: Imran’s camel broke loose.\n\nImran chased it into the desert and later expressed regret that he had not remained to hear the Prophet’s full explanation.\n\nThe chapter ends by presenting this regret as a lesson: knowledge can vanish in a moment, and some doors close once missed.\n\n## The journey continues\n\nThis chapter is the beginning of a longer path.\n\nFrom here onward, the series continues upward and inward—using the Qur’an, reports, and the language of modern discovery to explore the mysteries of the universe, the unseen, and the destiny of creation.\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
  "intro-ch-1": "# Welcome\n\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.\n\nAs-salamu Alaikum.\n\n## Why this app exists\n\nThis app is for everyone—but it’s built with Muslims in mind, because it’s essential that we know our own history, and the context behind it.\n\nAnd it isn’t only for Muslims. I urge everyone to read this, reflect on it, and understand.\n\nFrom today onward, I’m going to tell you a tale.\n\n## A true story of 40,000 years\n\nA true story of 40,000 years.\n\nThis story will be about the birth of the Earth and the skies—and also the beginning of life.\n\nWhere did the human come from? What has happened in the past? What is happening now? And what will happen in the future?\n\nStarting from today, one by one, I will present this story to you—beginning from the very start of everything, and going all the way to the end of everything.\n\n## How we’ll explore it\n\nI will present this story from every angle:\n\n- the angle of religion,\n- the angle of science,\n- and the angle of all useful knowledge that helps us understand this true story.\n\nTo understand this story, our biggest source will be a book in which there are no lies—a person who had never lied.\n\nWe will talk about those scientific sources that are confirmed—and not just theories or guess-work.\n\nOur story will include [[Archaeology|archaeology]]: evidence excavated from the ancient past—through which I will paint a picture of 40,000 years in front of you.\n\nWe will study the books and [[tablets|ancient-tablets]] written 5,000 years ago, which will help us understand those eras and periods.\n\n## The purpose\n\nAnd the purpose of telling you this story is to use all knowledge—and reach Him who has written this beautiful story.\n\nEvery chapter of this story will be an attempt to know Him, whose order will be visible—spread through these 40,000 years.\n\n## A humble disclaimer\n\n> “This is based on my own research and understanding. I could be wrong in places, but I used the best sources within my knowledge. Allah knows best.”\n\n## Support and share\n\nPlease support this app, and spread it among our brothers and sisters.\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
  "jinnat-in-islam-ch4": "# Jinnat in Islam\n\nIn every age, people have argued about what they cannot see. Some treat the unseen as superstition; others blame it for everything. This chapter returns to revelation and transmitted reports to describe what Islam says about the [[Jinn|jinn]]—their reality, their variety, and how their story appears in human memory.\n\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.\n\n## A belief that divides people\n\nAcross cultures, the idea of hidden beings appears again and again—yet people respond in opposite ways.\n\n- In some places, harmful jinn are blamed for every misfortune.\n- In other places, jinn are dismissed as fiction.\n\nSome skeptics have claimed that “jinn” was simply an ancient name for things like viruses and bacteria—unknown to earlier people. Others treated jinn as nothing more than a label for evil disbelievers.\n\nBut this chapter keeps to what the Islamic sources actually describe.\n\n## The sources for this chapter\n\nThe primary sources are the Qur’an and Hadith. Later sections also mention how the idea of jinn appears in historical memory.\n\nA statement repeated in this content is that modern detection is limited—only a tiny fraction of the universe is directly seen or measured. For the unseen, the chapter therefore relies on revealed guidance and transmitted narration.\n\n## The roadmap\n\nThis topic is introduced in four segments:\n\n1. The existence of jinn, their kinds, and how they appear  \n2. The difference between a jinn and a demon—and why “demons” are not identical to jinn  \n3. A powerful jinn described as exceptionally long-lived and influential  \n4. A jinn described as being with humans closely through life\n\nThis chapter begins with the foundations: what jinn are, how they are described, and how “demons” are framed in Islamic language.\n\n## What the word “jinn” means\n\nThe Arabic root points to something hidden or unseen. The chapter links this to other words built from the same sense of concealment—such as an embryo hidden in the womb.\n\nBut the jinn described here are not metaphors. They are presented as living beings: they eat, they move, and they can interact with the human world.\n\n## What jinn are created from\n\nThe Qur’an describes the jinn as created from fire—described in different expressions across the text.\n\nA report attributed to Ayesha (r.a) is cited as describing:\n- angels created from light,\n- the jinn created from fire,\n- and humans created from what has been described in revelation.\n\nThe point emphasized is difference of composition: jinn are not human, and they are not angels.\n\n## How do jinn look?\n\nA simple question—“What do jinn look like?”—does not have a simple answer.\n\nJust as animals vary wildly in form, jinn are presented as varied in kind and appearance. The chapter emphasizes that their “true” appearance is not something the human eye reliably captures. What people encounter, if they encounter anything at all, may be a form taken for interaction rather than an unveiled reality.\n\n## Listening in the sky and the valley of Nakhla\n\nThe chapter describes a change after the Prophet ﷺ was given prophethood: jinn were prevented from hiding and listening in the sky, and flames were thrown at those who attempted it.\n\nIt then describes a group of jinn travelling and passing through the valley of Nakhla, where they heard Qur’anic recitation. They recognized that something had changed, and they returned to their people with what they had heard.\n\nThis event is linked in the chapter to [[Surah al-Jinn|Surah Jinn]].\n\n## “The Night of the Jinn”\n\nA report attributed to [[Abdullah ibn Masud|abdullah-ibn-masud]] (r.a) is cited as describing a night when the Prophet ﷺ was not found among his companions.\n\nThey searched valleys and mountains, fearing the worst. Near dawn, the Prophet ﷺ was seen descending from the cave of Hira. The report describes that jinn had visited him and invited him to go with them to preach.\n\nThe narration also describes that the Prophet ﷺ later showed traces—footprints and signs of their fire.\n\nAnother report is mentioned in which the Prophet ﷺ is said to have been alerted by an acacia tree. The narration, as presented here, does not make clear whether this was the same night or a separate visit—only that jinn visited the Prophet ﷺ on more than one occasion.\n\n## Jinn appearing as snakes, dogs, and other forms\n\nThe chapter then discusses encounters where jinn are described as appearing in animal-like forms.\n\nA report attributed to [[Ibn Abbas|ibn-abbas]] (r.a) is cited as describing serpents as a deformed kind among the jinn—compared in the narration to earlier deformation stories.\n\nA longer incident is also narrated involving Abu Saeed Khudri (r.a) and his nephew, during the days of the battle of Ahzab. The nephew returned home, found his wife at the doorway, and then found a snake inside the house. The narration describes an exchange where the snake and the man were both harmed, and it remained unclear which struck first.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ is then quoted advising restraint: some jinn have become Muslims, and if something like this is encountered, it should be warned repeatedly before harm is done.\n\nAnother report is cited about a man guarding a date-farm, noticing dates disappearing, and encountering a being who appeared youthful—who identified itself as jinn. The narration describes the hands as resembling the paws of a dog, with fur, and the being claimed strength among its kind.\n\nThe chapter concludes this segment with the idea that certain crawling animals—snakes, scorpions, lizards—appear repeatedly in narrations connected to jinn encounters.\n\nIt also mentions a widely discussed modern incident in Brazil where a whale carcass was reported deep in a forest. The chapter presents it as an example of how strange events can provoke questions—without claiming a certain explanation.\n\n## Prophet Sulayman (a.s), manuscripts, and named jinn\n\nThe chapter turns to Prophet Sulayman (a.s), described as having authority over jinn.\n\nIt references verses from Surah Sa’ad describing jinn under Sulayman’s command—building, diving, and being restrained.\n\nIt then cites a chain of historical references to manuscripts and literature that list named jinn associated with Solomon’s court, describing unusual appearances and assigned powers. Several names and descriptions are mentioned as examples, alongside repeated caution: how much is true is ultimately known only to Allah.\n\nThe segment returns to the Qur’anic frame: the reason these references are mentioned is that the Qur’an describes jinn under Sulayman (a.s) performing work—construction and diving—echoed in some historical accounts.\n\nIt also mentions Ibn Kathir’s commentary describing a jinn named “Sakhar,” and later literature describing punishment and imprisonment in the depths of the sea, along with a reference to a “black king” in a book of wonders.\n\n## “Reports of [[Khurafa|khurafa]]” and jinn listening to Qur’an\n\nThe chapter notes that close contact between humans and jinn is discussed in narrations.\n\nA hadith attributed to Ayesha (r.a) is cited: one of the Prophet’s wives responded to a story by saying it sounded like one of the reports of [[Khurafa|khurafa]]. The narration explains “Khurafa” as a man captured by jinn in youth, held for a long time, then released—after which he told strange accounts that became known by that phrase.\n\nAnother scene is described where a group of jinn came to learn from the Prophet ﷺ, asked questions, and listened to recitation—specifically Surah Rahman with its repeated refrain. The jinn responded, and the Prophet ﷺ is quoted as saying the jinn responded better than the humans who remained silent.\n\n## What jinn eat—and why certain things are forbidden\n\nThe chapter cites a narration where jinn asked about food.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ is quoted describing bones from animals slaughtered in Allah’s name as provision for them, and describing excrement as provision for their animals.\n\nThis section is presented as part of how Islamic narrations describe an unseen ecosystem with its own rules.\n\n## Ma’arij, [[Samoom|samoom]], and stronger classes of jinn\n\nThe Qur’an’s language for the jinn’s composition is discussed using [[Ma'arij|ma'arij]] and [[Samoom|samoom]].\n\nThe chapter emphasizes that the true nature of this “fire” is not fully grasped.\n\nIt then cites a classification attributed to [[Al-Jahiz|al-jahiz]] in *The Book of Animals*:\n- a being that does both good and bad is described here as jinn,\n- a stronger and faster type is linked to “ma’arij,”\n- and an even stronger class is described as [[Ifrit|ifrit]].\n\nThe chapter connects the term “ifrit” to the court of Solomon and to the Qur’anic story of an ifrit claiming the ability to bring the throne of the Queen of Sheba swiftly.\n\nIt also cites a remark attributed to Sheikh Kishk, describing that some invisible jinn, if seen, would overwhelm the human understanding of beauty.\n\nFrom here, the chapter shifts into the subject of demons.\n\n## Demons vs. jinn—and what [[Shaytan|shaytan]] means\n\nThe chapter states that many scholars differentiate between jinn and demons.\n\nThe Qur’an addresses jinn and humans together, and it also speaks of “demons” as enemies of prophets—among both humans and jinn. In this framing, “demon” is not limited to one species.\n\nA hadith attributed to Hudhaifa bin Yaman (r.a) is cited describing leaders who will not follow the Prophet’s path, with bodies of humans and hearts of demons.\n\nThe focus here, however, is the demonic class among jinn: those defined by persistent evil behavior.\n\nThe Arabic word used is [[Shaytan|shaytan]]. The chapter notes meanings tied to “enemy/adversary” and to straying.\n\n## Before humans: jinn, demons, and [[Hins and Bins|hins-and-bins]]\n\nThe chapter then moves back in time.\n\nIt cites Qur’anic language about humans created from a black, echoing mud, and jinn created before them from intense fire.\n\nIt cites early scholarship describing the world inhabited by jinn and demons before humans, including a reference attributed to Abdullah ibn Umar (r.a) describing jinn roaming the earth long before Adam (a.s).\n\nIt also mentions a creature described in historical literature as [[Hins and Bins|hins-and-bins]]—presented as demonic souls—said to exist before jinn, then replaced. The chapter repeatedly cautions that this is not a hadith claim but appears in historical literature, and that certainty belongs to Allah alone.\n\nThe segment ends by mentioning that when jinn became rebellious, another jinn who lived among the angels is said to have been sent with angels to drive rebellious jinn to distant places—an idea the narration signals will be returned to later.\n\n## Demons in world cultures—and in hadith\n\nThe chapter notes that descriptions of demons appear across world cultures.\n\nIt cites Inuit traditions as containing thousands of demon descriptions, and it also cites Indian traditions as containing extensive demon classifications and even named demon genealogies.\n\nIt presents these as examples of how widespread the concept of hostile unseen beings is across civilizations.\n\n## A demon seen in the home—and the “black dog” form\n\nA report attributed to Qatadda bin Noman (r.a) is cited, describing a rainy night and a visit to Masjid an-Nabawi for prayer.\n\nAfter the prayer, the Prophet ﷺ asked what brought him at that hour. Qatadda explained he did not want the Prophet ﷺ to pray alone, as few would come in heavy rain.\n\nThe narration then describes the Prophet ﷺ giving him a small staff that lit his way, with angels accompanying him until he reached his home.\n\nOn the way, something in the form of a “black dog” tried to block him. The narration describes Qatadda striking it and continuing, then finding the Prophet ﷺ later and describing what happened.\n\nThe chapter presents this as part of a wider pattern: demonic encounters described in animal-like forms.\n\n## “The demon with the flame”—and a prayer of protection\n\nThe chapter then cites a report attributed to Abu Darda (r.a), describing an incident during prayer where the Prophet ﷺ sought refuge from a threatening presence and repeated words of rejection and curse.\n\nIt describes that the Prophet ﷺ later explained the presence as a demon approaching with a flame, and that Allah protected him.\n\nThe narration includes the idea of a protective supplication against the evil of shayateen.\n\n## Demons, the sky, and “stealing” information\n\nThe chapter then cites narrations describing shayateen influencing human states:\n- tying knots behind the head of a sleeping person,\n- sitting at the nostril of a sleeping person through the night.\n\nIt also returns to the earlier theme of the sky: demons attempting to ascend and “steal” information, and being struck away.\n\nThe chapter presents this as a framework for why fortune-telling and unseen claims can be mixed with falsehood—something stolen, then distorted.\n\n## “Shaytana,” and he-demons and she-demons\n\nThe chapter states that female demons are also referenced, using the term “shaytana.”\n\nIt cites a hadith collection where the word appears, and it cites a reported supplication before entering the washroom seeking refuge from he-demons and she-demons.\n\nThe point emphasized is breadth: the demonic category is not presented as singular or simplistic.\n\n## Demons in history and across civilizations\n\nThe chapter closes by noting that the concept of hostile unseen entities appears as far back as history itself.\n\nIt returns to the ancient world, referencing [[Mesopotamia|mesopotamia]] in modern Iraq and describing early civilizational memory of invisible entities harming humans.\n\nIt also mentions ancient Egyptian traditions describing “invisible ones” who mislead, and the existence of protective spells and practices designed to ward off harm.\n\nThe chapter ends on a sober note: regardless of how widely cultures describe demons, Islam anchors the discussion in revelation and in the moral lesson—seeking Allah’s protection, staying within what is permitted, and refusing to hand the unseen the power of obsession.\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
  "jinnat-in-islam-part-2-ch5": "# Iblees, the Qareen, and the Hidden War on the Human Heart\n\nThis chapter moves from the general world of jinn to the most personal battlefield: the human heart. It follows two connected realities described in Islamic sources—the sworn enemy who vowed to mislead humanity, and the unseen companion assigned to every person.\n\nIn the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.\n\n## The refusal that echoes through history\n\nThe Qur’an describes a moment that changed the story of humanity.\n\nAllah commanded the angels to prostrate in honor of Adam’s creation. All obeyed—except [[Iblees|iblees]].\n\nThis incident is mentioned repeatedly in the Qur’an. In this narration, Surah Kahf is referenced as clearly stating that [[Iblees|iblees]] was from the [[Jinn|jinn]].\n\nHe is described here as created from [[Samoom|samoom]]—and as the one who rebelled.\n\nBecause of this refusal, he was cast out, and he swore an oath: he would remain an enemy to humanity until the final day.\n\nThe narration also mentions linguistic and historical notes about his titles and names, presenting them as labels used across traditions.\n\n## The throne above water—and the oath against us\n\nA hadith is referenced stating that [[Iblees|iblees]] places his throne above water.\n\nThe chapter describes him as feeding on what is done without Allah’s name being remembered, and as hating humanity intensely.\n\nThe Qur’an records his argument and his vow:\n\n- that he would mislead humans,\n- that he would attack from every direction,\n- and that he would seek respite until the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe Qur’an also responds with a boundary: he has respite, but he is condemned, and he cannot dominate Allah’s servants—except those who choose to follow him.\n\nAlongside that, another reported statement is cited: as long as people continue to ask Allah for forgiveness, Allah continues to forgive.\n\nThe chapter places these two declarations side by side—one vow built on hatred, the other promise built on mercy.\n\n## The first strike: Adam (a.s.)\n\nThe narration describes [[Iblees|iblees]] beginning with the very first human.\n\nHis attack was not described as a chase, but as a suggestion—an invitation into desire:\n\n- eternal life,\n- a kingdom that never ends,\n- and elevated status.\n\nThe chapter emphasizes the method: he studies weakness.\n\nA hadith is referenced stating that when Adam’s body was created, it remained for a time, and [[Iblees|iblees]] circled it to understand what it was. When he realized it was hollow, he understood it would be vulnerable—susceptible to impulse, fear, and craving.\n\nThe chapter then connects that vulnerability to Adam and Hawwa being drawn into a mistake that led to their descent.\n\nIt also repeats a warning: he and his ranks observe humans from where humans cannot see them.\n\n## The strategy: traps, not pursuit\n\nIn this narration, [[Iblees|iblees]] is described as preferring traps over open confrontation.\n\nHe does not need to appear. He builds environments, moods, and pathways—then waits for the human to step into them.\n\nThe chapter describes the strongest moments of vulnerability as emotional extremes:\n- humiliation and inferiority\n- obsessive love or obsessive hatred\n- rage and despair\n- insecurity and lust\n\nIt also describes a final push at the edge of death—when the heart is weakest and fear is loudest.\n\n## The last attack near death\n\nA German book is referenced: [[Das Islamische Totenbuch|das-islamische-totenbuch]] (“The Islamic Book of the Dead”).\n\nIt is described here as containing reports of near-death experiences—some involving a figure offering water while trying to force words of disbelief.\n\nThe chapter treats these accounts as a pattern echoed across cultures: the enemy returns when the soul is departing, seeking a final collapse.\n\n## Echoes across cultures: worship, symbols, and confusion\n\nThe chapter then describes how the figure of the devil appears differently across civilizations—sometimes feared, sometimes honored.\n\nIt references ancient Mesopotamian memory of harmful demons and then contrasts how the same names or symbols can appear in other lands with altered meanings.\n\nIt references [[Ahura Mazda|ahura-mazda]] and mentions groups described as preserving unusual demon-related traditions, including [[Aghoris|aghoris]].\n\nIt also references a musician, [[Giuseppe Tartini|giuseppe-tartini]], and a piece associated with the idea of a “devil’s gift,” titled [[The Devil’s Trill|devils-trill]].\n\nThe chapter’s point is not to authenticate every story. It is to show how temptation can mirror a person’s deepest hunger—power, pride, lust, fame, rebellion—and how the same enemy can be dressed in different masks.\n\n## The Yazidis and the “peacock king” narrative\n\nA community is referenced: [[Yazidis|yazidis]].\n\nThe chapter mentions a text titled [[The Book of Illumination|book-of-illumination]] and another titled [[Kiteba Cilwe|kiteba-cilwe]], describing a “peacock lord/king” figure framed as an angel who refused to bow to Adam.\n\nThe narration connects this symbol to ideas of renewal and immortality, and it also references a story found in other traditions that connects the peacock to temptation and entry into Eden.\n\nThe chapter presents these references as examples of how a single refusal—[[Iblees|iblees]] refusing to bow—can echo into many myth-forms across regions.\n\n## La Santa Muerte and Diabolos\n\nThe chapter also references [[La Santa Muerte|la-santa-muerte]] and mentions cultural festivals around “the day of the dead,” presenting it as another example of how death-symbols can become spiritual identity in some communities.\n\nIt also references the Greek term [[Diabolos|diabolos]] and notes similarities in how languages preserve “enemy” imagery.\n\nThe narration returns to the same conclusion: [[Iblees|iblees]] is not a metaphor for “bad.” He is described here as a real, powerful enemy whose main target is the human heart.\n\n## The whisper, forgetfulness, and the cure\n\nThe chapter emphasizes a key weapon: whispering into the heart, then feeding forgetfulness.\n\nIt references examples from the Qur’an where forgetfulness was cast into a person, changing the course of events.\n\nThe remedy described is direct: remembrance of Allah.\n\nA report is cited about a companion complaining of forgetting Qur’an. The Prophet ﷺ is described as placing his hand on the person’s chest and commanding the devil to leave—after which the companion reported strength in memorization.\n\nThe chapter’s lesson is clear: remembrance of Allah is presented as stronger than whispering.\n\n## Fire, punishment, and the mystery of Zim-Hareer\n\nThe chapter reflects on a question: if [[Iblees|iblees]] is created from fire, what punishment can fire be to fire?\n\nIt references a Qur’anic term: [[Zim-hareer|zim-hareer]]—described here as an unimaginable freezing cold.\n\nThe chapter presents this as a reminder: punishment is not limited to one sensation, and the unseen contains realities the mind cannot fully picture.\n\n## Ibn Sayyad and the throne above water\n\nA narration about [[Ibn Sayyad|ibn-sayyad]] is referenced.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ is described as encountering him and asking about what he “saw.” He responded with a statement about a throne floating above water.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ is quoted in the narration as interpreting it as the throne of [[Iblees|iblees]].\n\nThe exchange ends with the Prophet ﷺ describing confusion around him and instructing to leave him.\n\nThe chapter uses this to reinforce a theme: the enemy is active, and his system is described as organized—built on deception and distortion.\n\n## The companion: Hamzad and Qareen\n\nNow the chapter turns to what is closest.\n\nThe narration introduces “invisible companion” language found across cultures, using the term [[Hamzad|hamzad]] and connecting it to the Arabic term [[Qareen|qareen]].\n\nIt references old literature where the concept of an unseen companion appears, and it also references the pre-Islamic Arabic poetic tradition called the [[Mu’allaqat|muallaqat]], where “qareen” language appears as a shadow of fear and companionship.\n\nThe point: the concept is ancient—but Islam gives it clearer shape.\n\n## Qur’anic framing of the Qareen\n\nThe chapter references verses where a person in Paradise says he had a “qareen” who used to mock the idea of the afterlife—then asks to see where that companion is, and finds him in punishment.\n\nIt then references another Qur’anic framing with stronger clarity: when someone turns away from remembrance, a demon is assigned as their [[Qareen|qareen]]—misleading while the person assumes they are guided.\n\nThe narration highlights a final line of regret:\n\nA person will wish there were the distance of east and west between them and their [[Qareen|qareen]].\n\n## Evidence that the Qareen is from the jinn\n\nA hadith is cited from [[Abdullah ibn Masud|abdullah-ibn-masud]] (r.a):\n\nEvery human is assigned a companion from the jinn.\n\nThe companions asked whether the Prophet ﷺ also had one. The Prophet ﷺ is quoted as saying yes—but Allah gave him mastery, and that companion accepted Islam and no longer advised him toward evil.\n\nThe chapter treats this as the core claim: the qareen exists, it is from the jinn, and its work is persuasion.\n\n## Possession—and the need to distinguish illness\n\nThe chapter then addresses a sensitive question: can jinn possession happen?\n\nIt describes the primary method as psychological pressure—casting thoughts, stirring fear, intensifying shame, and pushing toward sin—especially in moments of vulnerability:\n- extreme joy\n- severe stress\n- fear and anger\n- and the transition between sleep and wake\n\nBut it then gives a strict warning: it is crucial not to mislabel medical illness.\n\nA severe psychological condition is referenced: [[Dissociative Identity Disorder|dissociative-identity-disorder]]. The chapter describes it as involving multiple “alters,” shifts in identity, and episodes of memory gaps—often tied to childhood trauma.\n\n### Two ways the narration suggests distinguishing\n\n1) **Memory gaps**: significant missing time can occur in D.I.D because one identity does not retain what another experienced.  \n2) **Voice and expression changes**: the chapter describes dramatic changes in voice and behavior that can be misread as possession.\n\nThe point is protective: do not confuse medical illness with unseen influence, and do not ignore medical help when needed.\n\n## Who becomes more vulnerable—and why lying matters\n\nThe chapter then asks why some people seem more affected than others.\n\nIt references Qur’anic language about devils approaching liars and corrupt people, then connects that to a modern research reference.\n\nA 2016 research reference from [[University College London|university-college-london]] is mentioned regarding lying and emotional response, focusing on the [[Amygdala|amygdala]] and how repeated lying can reshape emotional reactions.\n\nThe chapter uses this as a bridge: when shame and fear are dulled, sinful thoughts become easier, and the inner doors become less guarded.\n\n## Five methods of protection\n\nThe chapter closes with protective guidance, presented as stronger than any attack.\n\n### 1) Do not feed the devil with fear\nA narration is cited where cursing the devil is discouraged, because it can inflate the devil’s sense of power. Instead, remembrance of Allah is described as shrinking his influence.\n\n### 2) Protection after nightmares\nA supplication is described for seeking refuge from Allah’s wrath and from the whispers of demons, especially after frightening dreams.\n\n### 3) Protection from harm and the evil eye\nA supplication is referenced that the Prophet ﷺ used for his grandsons, seeking refuge from devils, poisonous creatures, and harmful envy.\n\n### 4) Protection from the devil’s touch at death\nA supplication is referenced that includes seeking protection from dying in certain calamities—and specifically from the devil’s disturbance at the moment of death.\n\n### 5) The last two verses of Surah Al-Baqarah\nA hadith is cited describing the special protection associated with the closing verses of Surah Al-Baqarah—described as a shield for the home against devils and harm.\n\nThe chapter includes a translated rendering of those verses as part of its closing emphasis on mercy, forgiveness, and reliance on Allah.\n\n## A closing scene: Abdullah ibn Masud (r.a)\n\nThis chapter began and ended with [[Abdullah ibn Masud|abdullah-ibn-masud]] (r.a).\n\nIt references a story from his early life: how he refused to betray trust, how a miracle occurred with milk from a she-goat, and how that moment became a doorway into Islam.\n\nIt mentions that he learned many surahs directly from the Prophet ﷺ and became a major transmitter of Qur’an and hadith.\n\nThe chapter closes by returning to the two oaths:\n- the enemy swearing to attack as long as the soul remains,\n- and Allah promising forgiveness as long as forgiveness is sought.\n\nWith that, the fear of demons becomes smaller—and the love of Allah, His Messenger ﷺ, and the path of remembrance becomes stronger.\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
  "seven-earths-in-islam-ch6": "# Seven Earths in Islam\n\nWe think we know “earth” because we can stand on it. But the Qur’an speaks about earth with a scope that feels far larger than a single planet. This chapter explores what “earth” can mean—linguistically, physically, and spiritually—until it reaches the mysterious verse: **seven earths, “similar to them.”**\n\n## What “earth” really means\n\nIn everyday speech, “earth” means the land beneath our feet. But in technical terms, it includes far more than soil: minerals, gases, liquids—and the compounds without which life would not exist.\n\nThose life-sustaining compounds are described as [[Organic matter|organic-matter]].\n\nFrom here, the chapter asks a question that changes the scale:\n\n**How far does “earth” actually extend?**\n\n## A name that echoes across languages\n\nMany languages have words for “land,” but one root appears again and again in recognizable forms. The chapter highlights the Arabic word [[al-Ard|al-ard]] and its echoes across languages.\n\nAcross ancient and modern tongues, “earth” often points to the same idea:\nanything that is not air or water—and can be stood upon.\n\nBut if Allah swears by **the earth** and its expansion, is that oath only about the continents we know?\n\nOr is “earth” a category that reaches beyond what the eye can see?\n\n## Basalt and the first expansion of “earth”\n\nThe chapter presents a physical doorway into that larger meaning: rock.\n\nIt describes the earth’s crust as being dominated by [[Basalt|basalt]], and then follows basalt outward to places beyond our world.\n\n### The Moon and the black plains\n\nThe black plains on the Moon are known as [[Lunar maria|lunar-maria]]—vast basalt regions.\n\nIn this chapter’s framing, that makes the Moon’s “land” another face of “earth,” because it shares the same rocky substance.\n\n### Venus and the Venera missions\n\nThe bright “star” that appears at dawn and dusk is not a star—it is [[Venus|venus-planet]].\n\nThe chapter references the [[Venera missions|venera-missions]] and presents Venus as another basalt-heavy world wrapped in thick layers of cloud.\n\n### Mars and what is missing\n\n[[Mars|mars-planet]] is presented as another basalt-rich world.\n\nIn this framing, what separates our world from Mars is not stone—it is what our planet contains in abundance: water and [[Organic matter|organic-matter]].\n\n## Beyond the solar system: earth as a vast category\n\nThe chapter then widens the lens.\n\nIt describes [[Jupiter|jupiter-planet]] as a storm-covered giant with many moons, and it presents those moons as further “parts of earth” within this expanded definition.\n\nThen it steps beyond the solar system: thousands of earth-like planets have been discovered, and thousands more are visible but uncatalogued.\n\nTwo examples are highlighted:\n\n- [[Kepler-186f|kepler-186f]] — described here as a planet almost like our earth  \n- [[HD 100546|hd-100546]] — described here as a massive world, almost sun-sized in scale  \n\nThe chapter notes that these discoveries accelerated after 1990, with the count rising rapidly over time.\n\nThe conclusion is not a measurement—it is a humility:\n\n**Allah knows how far “earth” has been expanded.**\n\n## Earth in the Qur’an: creation, shaping, and spreading\n\nWith the scale now widened, the chapter revisits Qur’anic descriptions of earth:\n\n- earth being separated from the skies\n- earth taking form before the skies\n- earth being created and then expanded\n- mountains being placed upon earth\n- earth being filled with provision for its dwellers\n\nTo give a visible example of “mountains on earth,” the chapter compares mountains across worlds:\n\n- [[Mount Everest|mount-everest]] on our planet  \n- [[Mount Olympus|mount-olympus]] on Mars, described here as vastly larger  \n\nProvision is also framed as part of earth’s blessing. The chapter references resources known on our planet—like gas and oil—then points to Saturn’s moon [[Titan|titan-moon]] as being described with immense stores of similar substances.\n\nThe main idea remains consistent:\n**our planet is not “the whole earth.” It is one part of earth.**\n\n## Why was earth spread out?\n\nThe chapter then shifts from “what” to “why.”\n\nIt presents Qur’anic verses as describing earth being spread out for living beings—living beings of many kinds, in groups, in pairs, in diverse forms and colors—each with provision suited to their environment.\n\nIt also emphasizes:\n- Allah provides for those creatures\n- their distribution across earth is a sign\n- all dwellers of earth are under Allah’s rule\n- they praise Allah in ways we cannot comprehend\n\nWithin this framing, the chapter treats “earth” as a dwelling-space for creations beyond humans—creations Allah knows, counts, and can gather whenever He wills.\n\n## Our planet: from a harsh beginning to running waters\n\nThe chapter then returns to the part of earth we know personally: our world.\n\nIt presents the age of the planet as roughly 4.5 billion years and references ancient [[Zircon crystals|zircon-crystals]] as evidence used in that timeline.\n\nEarly earth is described as violent and sterile—bombarded by massive impacts—until conditions shifted.\n\nAn atmosphere formed, water condensed, and rain began to fall.\n\nThe oceans formed, and the chapter connects their continued liquidity to Allah stabilizing earth and giving it “running waters.” It presents this stability as necessary because water is the cradle of life:\n\n> Every living thing is created from water.\n\n## What is life?\n\nThe chapter acknowledges that definitions vary, then presents a simple working definition:\n\nA living thing is described as something that can:\n1) perform internal chemical reactions (metabolism)  \n2) repair itself  \n3) replicate itself (reproduction)\n\nHow life began is presented here as unknown to humanity—despite models, theories, and experiments—then framed through the Qur’anic lens: Allah brings life from what is not alive and revives earth after its death.\n\n## Early evidence of life—and a clue from deep space\n\nThe chapter describes ancient fossil evidence and suggests that early life appeared widely.\n\nIt then highlights a striking detail: an amino acid, [[Glycine|glycine]], was detected on [[Comet Wild 2|comet-wild-2]].\n\nWithin this chapter’s framing, this becomes a sign:\nlife’s building blocks can appear beyond our world—hinting that somewhere, on some distant part of earth, life could still be forming.\n\n## New realms—and the five great endings\n\nThe chapter describes early life as microbial and then shifts into the appearance of larger life—animals with muscles, nerves, and complex bodies.\n\nIt ties this idea to a Qur’anic theme: Allah replacing a people with others and creating in realms unknown.\n\nThen it lists a long parade of vanished worlds—giants and strange creatures—followed by the repeated reset of history:\n\nthe chapter describes five major global endings, known as [[Mass extinctions|mass-extinctions]].\n\nIt presents some as near-total collapses, and it links the rise of grass to a turning point that reshaped life on the surface.\n\n## The seven earths: “similar to them”\n\nAt the center of the chapter is a verse from Surah at-Talaq:\n\nAllah created seven skies—and [[Seven Earths|seven-earths]] similar to them—and His rule applies everywhere between them.\n\nThe chapter then supports this with a hadith warning against seizing land unjustly, describing burial to the depths of seven earths.\n\nThe question follows naturally:\n\n**What are these seven earths like? Where are they?**\n\nThe chapter answers cautiously:\nwe only know one earth the way we only know one kind of life.\n\n## Heraclius and the question of space\n\nThe chapter then narrates a question attributed to the Byzantine ruler [[Heraclius|heraclius]].\n\nHe challenges the idea of a paradise as vast as earth and sky, arguing that such scale leaves no “space” for Hell.\n\nThe answer given is brief and sharp:\nWhere does night go when day arrives?\n\nThe point is not to map coordinates. It is to break the assumption that reality must be understood only through the geometry of our limited perception.\n\n## Ibn Abbas and the boundary of explanation\n\nA question about seven earths is then brought to [[Ibn Abbas|ibn-abbas]] (r.a).\n\nHis reply is presented as a boundary:\nif their nature is explained, people may not believe—and that denial would become rejection.\n\nThe chapter also cites a report attributed to Ibn Abbas through Imam Bayhaqi describing that each earth could have equivalents of major prophetic histories “like yours.”\n\nWhether accepted, debated, or approached cautiously, the purpose in the chapter remains the same:\n**the unseen is vast, and human certainty has a limit.**\n\n## Different earths, different composition\n\nEarlier in the series, the skies were described as uniquely composed.\n\nHere, the chapter extends that idea:\nif the skies differ, the earths “similar to them” may also differ—by composition, by conditions, and by the kinds of life suited to them.\n\nA report is then mentioned from Abdullah bin Abi Dunya describing a distant earth to the west, bright in glow, whose inhabitants never disobey Allah and are unaware of the devil and even unaware of Adam’s story.\n\nThe chapter notes that chains can be debated—then asks the larger question it wants the reader to carry:\n\n**Is such variety truly impossible for the Creator of the heavens and the earth?**\n\n## Khaliq and Khallaq: the One who creates again and again\n\nThe chapter ends with two attributes:\n\n- [[Khaliq|khaliq]] — the One who creates  \n- [[Khallaq|khallaq]] — the One who originates creation repeatedly, again and again  \n\nIf the creation of skies and earths is greater than the creation of man, then creating beyond what we know is not difficult for Allah.\n\nThe chapter closes by stacking scale upon scale—earth and skies, the space between, and then what lies beyond—until the only honest ending remains:\n\n**Allahu Akbar.**  \n**Allah is the Greatest.**\n\n## Sources & Notes\n\n- **Qur'an:** Verses referenced in the chapter (readers can verify via any standard mushaf/translation).\n- **Hadith:** Where hadith are referenced, they are intended from the major collections; readers should verify grading and wording in primary sources.\n- **Classical scholarship:** Tafsīr and history works are used for framing and transmitted reports (details may vary between narrations).\n- **Note:** Where accounts differ, this chapter treats details as *reported* rather than absolute, and focuses on agreed-upon meaning and lessons.",
};
